    tldr: "Bayesian analysis combines a starting probability model with study data to update uncertainty about a specified effect. Its conclusions depend on the model and assumptions.",
    summary: "Bayesian inference combines a prior distribution and likelihood to obtain a posterior distribution for specified parameters, such as a treatment effect. A posterior probability or credible interval is conditional on those assumptions. Predictive probability averages over uncertainty to describe a specified future observation or trial outcome. These are different from a p-value, an individual response probability or guaranteed treatment benefit. Bayesian inference and adaptive design are separate choices: either can be used without the other. Informative borrowing requires relevant external data and sensitivity assessment; a minimally informative prior does not guarantee that every analysis equals a conventional one.",
    related: ["seamless-adaptive", "response-adaptive-randomisation", "dose-escalation-design", "external-control-arm", "basket-trial", "p-value", "confidence-interval", "statistical-significance"],
    trials: ["nmtrc003"],
