    tldr: "An estimand is a precise statement of what question a trial is answering, including what to do about patients who switch treatment, stop early or start another drug; the ICH E9(R1) framework makes trials write this down before they start.",
    summary: "An estimand precisely describes the treatment effect a trial intends to estimate. ICH E9(R1) specifies treatment conditions, target population, outcome variable, handling of relevant intercurrent events and a population-level summary. Stopping treatment, switching or receiving additional therapy can change the clinical question; they do not automatically justify deleting a participant. Death may prevent a later measurement from existing, whereas loss to follow-up can leave a relevant outcome missing. Choose the estimand before the estimator and align collection, assumptions and analysis to that question. A sensitivity analysis targets the same estimand under alternative assumptions; an analysis targeting a different effect is supplementary.",
