  asOf: "2026-11-12",
  notes: [
    "A treatment-policy strategy uses relevant outcomes regardless of the specified intercurrent event, such as adding another therapy. A hypothetical strategy instead asks about an explicitly defined alternative scenario; its answer requires appropriate assumptions and estimation. A composite can count death or another intercurrent event as part of failure. While-on-treatment strategies restrict the outcome question to the period before that event. Principal-stratum questions concern groups defined by potential intercurrent events, not just the observed adherent subset. Different events can need different strategies in one estimand.",
    "Distinguish the target effect, method and missing data. Treatment discontinuation does not necessarily stop outcome collection. Death is not simply a missing later symptom score when that score no longer exists. Keeping assigned groups and collecting follow-up are important, but an analysis-population label alone does not fully specify the estimand. Comparisons after switching, excluding switchers and hypothetical no-switch estimates need their own questions and assumptions; they cannot be ranked by a blanket rule that one is always truer."
  ]
